tor using everyday language, making complex SQL queries simple and intuitive."},"3443":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-17","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#build-a-custom-mcp-server-to-query-supabase","content":"This is a practical example of extending Pochi using the Model Context Protocol (MCP), and illustrates how teams can integrate AI into internal workflows for powerful, context-aware automation. This is particularly useful when you're debugging code, collaborating across teams, or building features that require quick access to live data (e.g: checking whether a migration seeded correctly, inspecting a table schema, or verifying specific column values)."},"3444":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-18","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#prerequisites","content":"Before we start, you’ll need the following:"},"3445":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-19","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#prerequisites","content":"Install Pochi"},"3446":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-20","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#prerequisites","content":"Setup a Supabase Project"},"3447":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-21","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#prerequisites","content":"OpenAI API Key  (You can use any other LLM model if preferred.)"},"3448":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-22","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#what-is-mcp-model-context-protocol","content":"MCP (Model Context Protocol) is a lightweight, flexible interface that allows large language models (LLMs) like those used in Pochi to connect and interact with external tools, APIs, or databases. In this tutorial, MCP acts as the bridge between your natural language queries and the Supabase database, enabling Pochi to translate plain English questions into SQL queries using OpenAI, execute them, and even generate visualizations using Vega Charts."},"3449":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-23","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#what-is-mcp-model-context-protocol","content":"We’re going to use Supabase database and limit to read only queries, but you can always extend to perform CRUD operations too. Using MCP servers makes it easy to extend AI assistants with custom functionality and is a perfect way to keep your focus on coding while still getting the data context you need."},"3450":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-24","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-1-setup-your-vs-code-project","content":"First, we’ll set up a new project inside VS Code. To do so, we’ll create a new folder and initialise a Node project."},"3451":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-25","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-1-setup-your-vs-code-project","content":"The command npm init -y quickly creates a package.json file in your project directory using default values, without prompting you for configuration."},"3452":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-26","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-1-setup-your-vs-code-project","content":"Once the project is setup, we need to install the required dependencies. Run the following command:"},"3453":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-27","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-1-setup-your-vs-code-project","content":"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk – Provides tools to manage structured context and memory for LLMs."},"3454":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-28","page_i