/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-5-testing-the-mcp-server","content":"(make sure you’re in your project folder, in this case: supabase-mcp-server)."},"3485":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-59","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-5-testing-the-mcp-server","content":"Open the Inspector in your browser (it launches a local port)."},"3486":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-60","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-5-testing-the-mcp-server","content":"You’ll see a UI showing your server name (`supabase-mcp``)."},"3487":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-61","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-5-testing-the-mcp-server","content":"Under “Tools” you should see `querySupabaseSQL``."},"3488":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-62","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-5-testing-the-mcp-server","content":"Click it, pass in your input:"},"3489":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-63","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-5-testing-the-mcp-server","content":"You should see the correct response which includes the Supabase database query result and a Vega Lite JSON spec."},"3490":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-64","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-5-testing-the-mcp-server","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3491":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-65","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#enable-your-mcp-server","content":"Now that your MCP server is verified, you can enable it inside Pochi to use it in your editor’s natural language chat UI. For that, we need to go to Pochi’s Tools Settings page (you can also find it via Command Palette: Pochi: Open Tools Settings)"},"3492":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-66","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#enable-your-mcp-server","content":"Look for the MCP server named my-supabase-mcp and toggle it ON (enabled). You should see a green dot to indicate its connected."},"3493":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-67","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#enable-your-mcp-server","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3494":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-68","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-6-executing-the-mcp-server-in-pochi","content":"Once it works in Inspector, we can connect it into Pochi (our config already points to index.js). Then when you give Pochi a natural language prompt,  asking the model to call queryDatabase tool, it will answer all your database related queries easily."},"3495":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-69","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-6-executing-the-mcp-server-in-pochi","content":"Prompt used:"},"3496":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-70","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-6-executing-the-mcp-server-in-pochi","content":"Use the queryDatabase tool to find out what are the total sales for each product by region"},"3497":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-71","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-6-executing-the-mcp-server-in-pochi","content":"Here’s the final working demo:"},"3498":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-72","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-6-executing-the-mcp-server-in-pochi","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3499":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-73","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-6-executing-the-mcp-server-in-pochi","content":"You can also paste in the Vega - Lite JSON spec you receive in response into the Vega Editor and get a chart response of the requested query:"},"3500":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-74","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-7-optional-using-the-mcp-server-with-pochi-cli","content":"So far, we’ve tested our Supabase MCP server in the Inspector, wired it into the Pochi VS Code extension, and also tested via the Pochi chat UI. But Pochi also provides a CLI client that can connect to MCP servers directly from your terminal."},"3501":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-75","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-7-optional-using-the-mcp-server-with-pochi-cli","content":"This means you can query your database without opening VS Code at all:"},"3502":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-76","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-7-optional-using-the-mcp-server-with-pochi-cli","content":"Behind the scenes, the Pochi CLI works the same way as the VS Code extension: it connects to your MCP server via stdio, calls the queryDatabase tool, and streams the results back into your terminal."},"3503":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-77","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-7-optional-using-the-mcp-server-with-pochi-cli","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3504":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-78","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#step-7-optional-using-the-mcp-server-with-pochi-cli","content":"Invoking via the CLI helps if you spend most of your time in the shell or on remote servers. It also works great over SSH, in Docker containers, or CI/CD pipelines where you don’t have an editor UI. Moreover, you can use in automation workflows by wrapping pochi -p commands in scripts to periodically check metrics."},"3505":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-79","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#conclusion-improving-developer-workflow-for-teams-and-enterprises","content":"Whether you're querying user logs, verifying migrations, or building internal dashboards, MCP lets you build custom servers that connect Pochi to your unique internal tools and workflows. This Supabase MCP server is just one example of how Pochi’s powerful extensibility can help your team stay in flow, access real-time context, and reduce the friction of internal data access."},"3506":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-80","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#conclusion-improving-developer-workflow-for-teams-and-enterprises","content":"Now that you’ve seen what’s possible, here are a few ways to take your project further:"},"3507":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-81","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#conclusion-improving-developer-workflow-for-teams-and-enterprises","content":"Deploy your MCP server with HTTP Transport: Instead of running locally via stdio, you can deploy your server over HTTP. This enables multiple team members. from developers to engineering managers, to securely access the same database instance through Pochi, with proper access controls, rate limiting, and centralized schema management. Learn more"},"3508":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-82","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#conclusion-improving-developer-workflow-for-teams-and-enterprises","content":"Expand beyond read-only queries: This tutorial focused on read-only queries for simplicity, but you can extend your MCP server to support full CRUD operations, allowing your natural language interface to create, update, and delete records as well."},"3509":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-83","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#conclusion-improving-developer-workflow-for-teams-and-enterprises","content":"Customize and optimize prompts: Experiment with prompt design for OpenAI to improve SQL accuracy or add support for additional data visualizations beyond Vega Lite charts."},"3510":{"id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server-84","page_id":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/supabase-mcp-server#conclusion-improving-developer-workflow-for-teams-and-enterprises","content":"Contribute and share: If you built something cool or want to see tutorials on related topics, such as CRUD operations or deployment best practices, please reach out by using /create-issue command within the Pochi VS Code extension or contribute to the project!"},"3511":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"page","content":"Vibe Coding a macOS GitHub Client in Pochi","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app"},"3512":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-0","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","content":"Using a Coding Agent to build a macOS App in SwiftUI"},"3513":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-1","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"heading","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#vibe-coding-a-macos-github-client-in-pochi","content":"Vibe Coding a macOS GitHub Client in Pochi"},"3514":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-2","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"heading","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-1-describe-the-product-not-the-code","content":"Phase 1: Describe the product, not the code"},"3515":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-3","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"heading","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"Phase 2: The app launches, but no window appears"},"3516":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-4","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"heading","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"Phase 3: API indirection and permission boundaries"},"3517":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-5","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"heading","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"Phase 4: This technically works, but it’s not usable yet"},"3518":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-6","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"heading","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"Phase 5: Product-level feedback instead of UI code"},"3519":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-7","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"heading","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#some-ending-thoughts","content":"Some ending thoughts"},"3520":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-8","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#vibe-coding-a-macos-github-client-in-pochi","content":"title: TL;DR"},"3521":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-9","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#vibe-coding-a-macos-github-client-in-pochi","content":"This app was built entirely through a single Pochi task. You can view the full prompt history and agent actions here."},"3522":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-10","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#vibe-coding-a-macos-github-client-in-pochi","content":"I deal with a lot of GitHub notifications every day - issue activities, PR reviews, mentions/tags, and administrative notifications. Viewing this on GitHub leaves me with an interface that becomes messy real fast, with slow network requests any time I try to filter or group notifications."},"3523":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-11","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#vibe-coding-a-macos-github-client-in-pochi","content":"I wanted a faster way to deal with this on macOS. Ideally, something closer to a mailbox - you just fetch once, filter locally, and archive easily while only opening the browser when necessary."},"3524":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-12","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#vibe-coding-a-macos-github-client-in-pochi","content":"Instead of setting up a project manually, I went straight to Pochi and described the app I wanted to build."},"3525":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-13","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#vibe-coding-a-macos-github-client-in-pochi","content":"What followed was less about generating more UI code and more about dealing with the kinds of problems real desktop apps tend to run into."},"3526":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-14","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#vibe-coding-a-macos-github-client-in-pochi","content":"Here’s a walkthrough of five prompt phases I went through to build a working macOS app, all without touching a single line of code."},"3527":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-15","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-1-describe-the-product-not-the-code","content":"I started by describing the behavior I expected from the app:"},"3528":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-16","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-1-describe-the-product-not-the-code","content":"Given that description, Pochi generated a Swift Package Manager project using a fairly standard MVVM structure."},"3529":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-17","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-1-describe-the-product-not-the-code","content":"There was a service responsible for talking to the GitHub API and handling token storage, with a view model that owned both the notification list and the filtering logic. The UI would have a main content area, a column to display the list of notifications, and a sidebar for filters."},"3530":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-18","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-1-describe-the-product-not-the-code","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3531":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-19","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-1-describe-the-product-not-the-code","content":"It's quite impressive to see that the performance constraint was respected: filtering happened entirely in memory, and the network layer was fetched once and stayed out of the UI loop."},"3532":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-20","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"Following the initial implementation, the code compiled successfully. But no window appeared."},"3533":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-21","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"I checked for errors or crashes, but found none. Though it felt like a UI bug, I did what was left as the easiest thing to do: I described the problem back to Pochi."},"3534":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-22","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"After this, Pochi started inspecting the app entry point and build configuration. The problem turned out not to be a SwiftUI issue at all. There was a missing embedded Info.plist and no proper bundle identifier, which led to the application not activating itself when launched. That meant, even though the process existed, the app never came to the front."},"3535":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-23","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"To fix this, Pochi generated an Info.plist file, embedded it via linker settings in Package.swift, and added activation logic in the AppDelegate.swift so the application would finally present a window."},"3536":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-24","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3537":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-25","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"These are not things most people think about while writing SwiftUI code. On macOS, SwiftUI views are hosted inside an application process that has several system-level requirements."},"3538":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-26","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"This includes bundle metadata, activation policy, and lifecycle hooks. Xcode templates set all of this up automatically. But when you build a macOS app using Swift Package Manager directly, these details become your responsibility."},"3539":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-27","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-2-the-app-launches-but-no-window-appears","content":"The macOS platform constraints still apply, but I no longer had to hunt through configuration files or lifecycle hooks myself. I described the problem to Pochi, and the agent traced the issue through the app’s entry point and build configuration to find and fix the root cause autonomously."},"3540":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-28","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"Now that the window finally appeared, notifications started loading. However, clicking on them did not show much useful information."},"3541":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-29","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"When I tried to open some issues, the app started failing with an invalidResponse error. The URLs looked correct, and the code path was the same as for issues that worked, so it wasn’t obvious where the failure was coming from. My first assumption was that this was a parsing or decoding bug."},"3542":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-30","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3543":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-31","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"At this point, it’s worth noting that GitHub’s notifications API does not return issue or pull request content. It only returns lightweight objects that contain URLs pointing to the actual resources. To render the discussion, the app must follow that link, fetch the issue or PR, and then fetch comments from yet another endpoint."},"3544":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-32","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"This also means that notification access and repository access are not the same thing. An API token may have permission to read notifications, while still lacking permission to read the underlying resource."},"3545":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-33","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"Instead of manually tracing the networking stack, I described the error back to Pochi and asked it to investigate."},"3546":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-34","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"Pochi did not start rewriting decoding logic. Instead, it treated the API as a black box and validated assumptions around the request itself. It tested the same URLs independently and found that the failing requests were returning 404."},"3547":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-35","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"That immediately ruled out JSON parsing issues and pointed to access control. The token had notifications scope, which allows reading notifications, but not repo scope, which is required to fetch issue and pull request details, especially for private repositories."},"3548":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-36","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"In other words, the fix was not in code at all. The token needed additional scopes. You usually don't encounter problems like these when dealing with isolated API examples, but in my case, it became apparent once authentication scopes and real user flows merged."},"3549":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-37","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-3-api-indirection-and-permission-boundaries","content":"Moreover, the interesting part was how the agent did not assume the error was a decoding bug that required rewriting networking code. Instead, it took a fresh approach and treated the API as a black box, validating the assumptions across access and permissions first."},"3550":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-38","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"Now that issues and pull requests were loading correctly, another limitation became obvious. The content was being rendered as plain text. In hindsight, this was expected, since GitHub content is Markdown."},"3551":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-39","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3552":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-40","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"But a plain text view of issues and pull requests is not very appealing. On all fronts, SwiftUI generated something readable, but it lost meaning once the returned data involved large chunks of text."},"3553":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-41","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"I'm throwing this problem back to Pochi again:"},"3554":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-42","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"I could observe the thought process as the agent tried to reason through possible approaches."},"3555":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-43","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"The first attempt was to use a Markdown rendering library to display the raw Markdown returned by the API. That was a reasonable first take, but it quickly ran into GitHub-specific quirks. GitHub doesn’t handle Markdown the same way most standard renderers do, largely because it allows embedded HTML, media links, and a number of formatting extensions."},"3556":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-44","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"I followed up with another prompt describing these issues:"},"3557":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-45","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"Following this, Pochi tried a second approach. It clearly wanted to avoid replicating GitHub’s rendering pipeline locally. Since GitHub can return rendered HTML for body content, this provided a way around dealing with all of GitHub’s Markdown extensions on the client. All the agent had to do was request the pre-rendered HTML and display it directly."},"3558":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-46","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"This switch improved things significantly and made the detail view look much closer to what users expect from the GitHub website."},"3559":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-47","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3560":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-48","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"Looking back, none of this was part of the original prompt. It came from keeping the agent’s options open while trying to make the notifications view more useful. Presenting a strict solution up front would likely have taken much longer to reach the same outcome."},"3561":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-49","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-4-this-technically-works-but-its-not-usable-yet","content":"After this change, the app started to look more and more like a lightweight GitHub client. It became possible to go through entire comment threads without leaving the app."},"3562":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-50","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"Once the app was visibly functional, the issues that remained were no longer technical. Instead, I started noticing what felt off from a UX perspective."},"3563":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-51","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"The list felt too narrow."},"3564":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-52","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"Icons did not distinguish between open, closed, and merged pull requests."},"3565":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-53","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"Filters did not indicate how many items they would return."},"3566":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-54","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"Archiving required selecting items and using a toolbar button, even when I already knew exactly which notifications I wanted to dismiss."},"3567":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-55","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"Each of these was easy to describe in words, but implementing them manually would have required non-trivial view model changes, state handling, and layout adjustments."},"3568":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-56","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"I never had to sit through and review the code. I just expressed product-level concerns about expected behavior and what didn't sit well after a few rounds of real use. The agent automatically translated the feedback into SwiftUI and view model updates."},"3569":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-57","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"For example, I told Pochi:"},"3570":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-58","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"From that description, the agent widened the list column, added state-aware icons for issues and pull requests, and introduced per-row archive actions. It also handled the necessary view model changes to support these interactions, without me touching any layout code or dealing with further prompting."},"3571":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-59","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"Here’s a look at the final demo:"},"3572":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-60","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#phase-5-product-level-feedback-instead-of-ui-code","content":"Your browser does not support the video tag."},"3573":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-61","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#some-ending-thoughts","content":"Most of the work on this project was not about inventing algorithms or designing architecture. It was about navigating constraints that already exist and wiring together pieces that do not quite fit by default. In that sense, the agent was not replacing development work so much as changing its shape."},"3574":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-62","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#some-ending-thoughts","content":"The code still had to satisfy macOS. The API still had to satisfy GitHub. Those requirements did not go away. They just became more apparent through conversation rather than through stack traces and breakpoints. And instead of sitting, editing these files, I was describing problems and desired behavior. It was quite surprising, how little of this felt like coding."},"3575":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-63","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#some-ending-thoughts","content":"If nothing else, building this app convinced me that evaluating coding agents only on greenfield generation misses most of the hard parts of software - permissions, lifecycle management, data modeling, and user-facing edge cases. The real test begins once the first version works and you start asking whether it actually fits into the world it is supposed to run in."},"3576":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-64","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#some-ending-thoughts","content":"Feel free to check out the full repository here."},"3577":{"id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app-65","page_id":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app","type":"text","url":"/tutorials/vibe-coding-github-macos-app#some-ending-thoughts","content":"You can also see the full trajectory of my interaction history with the agent here."}},"count":3577},"sorting":{"language":"english","sortableProperties":["content","page_id","type","keywords","tag","url"],"sortablePropertiesWithTypes":{"content":"string","page_id":"string","type":"string","keywords":"string","tag":"string","url":"string"},"sorts":{"content":{"docs":{"1":442,"2":2023,"3":443,"4":3265,"5":2878,"6":1768,"7":1769,"8":2317,"9":2697,"10":445,"11":660,"12":2204,"13":444,"14":2354,"15":2348,"16":1667,"17":2858,"18":1149,"19":3289,"20":2927,"21":1024,"22":1280,"23":703,"24":1681,"25":2926,"26":717,"27":1756,"28":1603,"29":2869,"30":2268,"31":1282,"32":1648,"33":2093,"34":1897,"35":2913,"36":3388,"37":591,"38":592,"39":1093,"40":2958,"41":2042,"42":2889,"43":2037,"44":2166,"45":593,"46":1852,"47":2086,"48":1903,"49":2859,"50":899,"51":302,"52":625,"53":629,"54":2806,"55":628,"56":594,"57":2044,"58":2043,"59":2045,"60":1676,"61":1272,"62":1279,"63":1888,"64":901,"65":2635,"66":1036,"67":1831,"68":546,"69":1488,"70":581,"71":888,"72":941,"73":3323,"74":617,"75":1849,"76":618,"77":1755,"78":441,"79":2912,"80":767,"81":1869,"82":2807,"83":2408,"84":741,"85":1293,"86":971,"87":2349,"88":995,"89":2466,"90":3376,"91":1771,"92":977,"93":2802,"94":2