rinciple for NES, we consistently choose the latter."],[2458,"At this point, the decision falls under either pulling the user away from where they’re working or bringing the preview to them. Since preserving developer flow is a core design principle for NES, we consistently choose the latter."],[2247,"Attach intent directly to code using inline comments."],[2855,"Attach intent directly to code using inline comments."],[1072,"Attachment Preview in Chat Input: File attachment previews now appear inside the chat input form rather than outside it."],[2047,"Attachment Preview in Chat Input: File attachment previews now appear inside the chat input form rather than outside it."],[1867,"Aug 29, 2025"],[1528,"August 16, 2026"],[1521,"August 22, 2026"],[1514,"August 30, 2026"],[509,"Authenticate with pochi auth, including support for Gemini login #167"],[2798,"Authenticate with pochi auth, including support for Gemini login #167"],[78,"Authentication"],[1,"Auto Compact"],[3,"Auto Compact"],[13,"Auto Compact becomes available from the token usage chip in the toolbar when all of the following are true:"],[10,"Auto Compact helps you keep long conversations efficient by summarizing earlier messages when your token usage grows large. You can either:"],[1149,"Auto Compact: Pochi now compacts your context automatically when it gets full mid-task, without stopping what it's doing. A \"Compacting...\" indicator appears in the thread while it runs, and the task picks up where it left off."],[1971,"Auto Compact: Pochi now compacts your context automatically when it gets full mid-task, without stopping what it's doing. A \"Compacting...\" indicator appears in the thread while it runs, and the task picks up where it left off."],[711,"Auto Layout: We added a new “Pochi Layout” toggle that instantly arranges your VS Code workspace into an optimized 3-pane layout: the Pochi task view on the left, code/diffs on the upper right, and terminals in a separate bottom-right tab group. This keeps terminal output isolated, prevents accidental layout shifts, and makes long-running tasks much easier to follow. #733"],[2636,"Auto Layout: We added a new “Pochi Layout” toggle that instantly arranges your VS Code workspace into an optimized 3-pane layout: the Pochi task view on the left, code/diffs on the upper right, and terminals in a separate bottom-right tab group. This keeps terminal output isolated, prevents accidental layout shifts, and makes long-running tasks much easier to follow. #733"],[1085,"Auto Memory + Auto Dream: We're building persistent memory across sessions — a MEMORY.md file that Pochi maintains over time."],[2060,"Auto Memory + Auto Dream: We're building persistent memory across sessions — a MEMORY.md file that Pochi maintains over time."],[471,"Auto-Approve Menu: This menu at the top of the zone allows you to grant Pochi permission to use tools autonomously, streamlining the workflow."],[1108,"Auto-Expand Task Panel Input: The task panel input now expands as you type, up to 30% of viewport height or 420px, before switching to internal scrolling."],[2023,"Auto-Expand Task Panel Input: The task panel input now expands as you type, up to 30% of viewport height or 420px, before switching to internal scrolling."],[512,"Autocomplete: Pochi suggests relevant tools, functions, and variables to help you type prompts faster. #89"],[2801,"Autocomplete: Pochi suggests relevant tools, functions, and variables to help you type prompts faster. #89"],[364,"Automatic Discovery"],[1032,"Available to all logged-in Pochi users. Supports up to 20 domains; allowlist and denylist modes cannot be mixed. #1457"],[2072,"Available to all logged-in Pochi users. Supports up to 20 domains; allowlist and denylist modes cannot be mixed. #1457"],[3100,"B. Select Your Transcription Engine\nHandy can run multiple local transcription models:"],[1175,"Background agents reuse the parent's system prompt: Pochi now reuses the parent's system prompt when it spins up a background agent, instead of rebuilding one from scratch. It captures the resolved prompt once the parent's first request finishes and passes it down, so the cached prefix on Anthropic and OpenAI stays warm."],[1950,"Background agents reuse the parent's system prompt: Pochi now reuses the parent's system prompt when it spins up a background agent, instead of rebuilding one from scratch. It captures the resolved prompt once the parent's first request finishes and passes it down, so the cached prefix on Anthropic and OpenAI stays warm."],[1264,"Background jobs will report back automatically: In Pochi 0.66, long-running commands will be able to continue in the background while Pochi moves on to other work. When a job finishes, Pochi will receive a completion notification, and the job and its output will remain attached to the task so it can inspect the result without repeatedly checking whether the command is done."],[1898,"Background jobs will report back automatically: In Pochi 0.66, long-running commands will be able to continue in the background while Pochi moves on to other work. When a job finishes, Pochi will receive a completion notification, and the job and its output will remain attached to the task so it can inspect the result without repeatedly checking whether the command is done."],[1176,"Background scheduling waits for that snapshot before anything spins off. Previously each background agent built its own prompt and broke the shared cache prefix."],[1951,"Background scheduling waits for that snapshot before anything spins off. Previously each background agent built its own prompt and broke the shared cache prefix."],[977,"Background subtask execution: Subtasks will run asynchronously in separate tabs without blocking the parent task, enabling smoother parallel workflows.  #1354"],[2147,"Background subtask execution: Subtasks will run asynchronously in separate tabs without blocking the parent task, enabling smoother parallel workflows.  #1354"],[854,"Background Subtasks: newTask can now be run asynchronously as a background child task. These subtasks preserve the parent–child relationship but do not block the parent task, allowing agents to delegate long-running work while continuing execution.#1022"],[2377,"Background Subtasks: newTask can now be run asynchronously as a background child task. These subtasks preserve the parent–child relationship but do not block the parent task, allowing agents to delegate long-running work while continuing execution.#1022"],[1306,"Background-job results will reach Pochi sooner: When a background command finishes during an active task, Pochi will receive the result at the next safe step instead of waiting until the turn ends. This will let it react to completed tests, builds, and other jobs while the result is still useful, without resuming a task you intentionally paused. #1932"],[1878,"Background-job results will reach Pochi sooner: When a background command finishes during an active task, Pochi will receive the result at the next safe step instead of waiting until the turn ends. This will let it react to completed tests, builds, and other jobs while the result is still useful, without resuming a task you intentionally paused. #1932"],[3069,"baseURL: Change this to the URL of your Tabby server instance."],[612,"Bash Commands in Workflows You can now execute Bash commands directly inside Pochi workflows using the ! prefix. This allows workflows to pull live system or repository context, like git status or uname -a, and feed the results into the model before running your task. \nFor example:"],[2733,"Bash Commands in Workflows You can now execute Bash commands directly inside Pochi workflows using the ! prefix. This allows workflows to pull live system or repository context, like git status or uname -a, and feed the results into the model before running your task. \nFor example:"],[2248,"Batch feedback into a single update instead of multiple chat messages."],[2856,"Batch feedback into a single update instead of multiple chat messages."],[3298,"Batch processing - Generating test reports, review reports for multiple modules"],[1443,"Because NES has the ability to propose structural edits, such as inserting a new helper function, refactoring a block of logic, or adding a multi-line configuration, it frequently produces multi-line suggestions that cannot be represented as simple, inline changes."],[2456,"Because NES has the ability to propose structural edits, such as inserting a new helper function, refactoring a block of logic, or adding a multi-line configuration, it frequently produces multi-line suggestions that cannot be represented as simple, inline changes."],[1439,"Because NES predicts the next meaningful edit across the file (not just at the cursor), many suggestions naturally apply outside the user’s current editing position. For example, while you are typing inside a function, NES may suggest updating a related import, adjusting a type definition, or fixing a reference several lines away."],[2452,"Because NES predicts the next meaningful edit across the file (not just at the cursor), many suggestions naturally apply outside the user’s current editing position. For example, while you are typing inside a function, NES may suggest updating a related import, adjusting a type definition, or fixing a reference several lines away."],[1379,"Because realistically, the next edit will almost always happen very close to where the user is already editing. This small window keeps the latency extremely low and is large enough to capture the structure around the edit."],[2596,"Because realistically, the next edit will almost always happen very close to where the user is already editing. This small window keeps the latency extremely low and is large enough to capture the structure around the edit."],[2261,"Before an agent attempts to solve a problem, it evaluates what it can do with every enabled tool. Each additional MCP server expands the action space the model